WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.020
Modern systems typically provide operating system or approved library APIs for security-grade random bytes.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:19.960
The exact API varies by platform, but the design principle is consistent: use the system or trusted library source intended for security-sensitive randomness rather than building your own generator.

3
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:29.120
Platform-specific APIs differ in names, blocking behavior, error reporting, initialization expectations, and supported environments.

4
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:35.700
C code should document which platforms are supported and which randomness source is used on each one.

5
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:41.000
This is especially important for portable libraries and cross-platform applications.

6
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.560
Random byte generation should check return values.

7
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:51.540
The code should confirm that the requested amount was produced, and it should define what happens when the call fails or produces less than requested.

8
00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.160
Security-sensitive values should not be created from incomplete output.

9
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.680
A small wrapper function can help.

10
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:06.420
A wrapper can centralize platform differences, enforce complete output, convert API errors into application errors, and make call sites consistent.

11
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.720
It also gives reviewers one place to inspect the security-grade randomness path.

12
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.160
Avoid mixing untrusted or predictable values into a secure random source unless the platform or library explicitly supports that operation safely.

13
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.580
Application-level mixing is easy to do badly and usually unnecessary.

14
00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.160
Request the bytes you need from the trusted source and keep the failure behavior simple.
